<main class="students"><nav class="crumb"><a href="/en/">English edition</a> / <a href="/en/library/">Popular library</a> / Study guides</nav><header><p class="eyebrow">OPEN STUDY LIBRARY · ENGLISH STUDY RESOURCES</p><h1>Martín Fierro <em>for Students</em></h1><p class="intro">Learn the story canto by canto without losing contact with José Hernández’s original Spanish words. Each published guide includes the plot, major voices, selected authentic verses, literary themes and questions for reflection.</p></header><section><h2>Part One — El gaucho Martín Fierro (1872)</h2><div class="cards">{lessons.map(x=><article><p class="chapter">CANTO {x.n}</p><h3><a href={`/en/students/canto-${x.n}/`}>{x.title}</a></h3><p>{x.desc}</p><a href={`/en/students/canto-${x.n}/`}>Read the study guide ↗</a> · <a href={`/leer/ida/${x.n}/`} lang="es">Original poem</a></article>)}</div><p class="note">Nine canto guides are now published, covering Cantos I–IX of the 1872 poem. Additional cantos will be linked only when their guides have been completed and verified against the original corpus.</p></section><section class="feature"><h2>Read across the whole poem</h2><p><a href="/en/library/old-vizcacha-vs-martin-fierro/">Old Vizcacha versus Martín Fierro</a> compares different approaches to life and morality using original passages from the sequel’s Cantos XV and XXXII. The <a href="/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/">complete advice to his sons</a> is also available with English explanations.</p><p><a href="/en/martin-fierro-summary/">Story summary</a> · <a href="/en/characters/">Main characters</a> · <a href="/en/library/themes-and-analysis/">Themes and analysis</a> · <a href="/en/magazine/frontier-forts/">Frontier history</a></p></section></main>
